\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.10}{\ignorespaces \emph  {Geodesics-in-heat approximation of the distance to a dense red non-convex source curve on a two-dimensional grid.} The first panel shows the exact Euclidean distance to the curve. The other panels diffuse a narrow band by the one-step approximation $(\mathrm  {Id}+\varepsilon L_h/4)^{-1}$ with Neumann boundary conditions, then recover a distance by the normalized-gradient Poisson solve. Each panel is robustly normalized, and contour values are selected from quantiles of the displayed field. Increasing the Sinkhorn temperature $\varepsilon $ suppresses unresolved grid-scale artifacts but progressively rounds the non-convex level-set geometry.}}{144}{figure.8.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sinkhorn-geodesics-in-heat}{{8.10}{144}{\emph {Geodesics-in-heat approximation of the distance to a dense red non-convex source curve on a two-dimensional grid.} The first panel shows the exact Euclidean distance to the curve. The other panels diffuse a narrow band by the one-step approximation $(\Id +\epsilon L_h/4)^{-1}$ with Neumann boundary conditions, then recover a distance by the normalized-gradient Poisson solve. Each panel is robustly normalized, and contour values are selected from quantiles of the displayed field. Increasing the Sinkhorn temperature $\epsilon $ suppresses unresolved grid-scale artifacts but progressively rounds the non-convex level-set geometry}{figure.8.10}{}}
